#{[17592186385847 #edb/ref 1080 "$3$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45ae9be6-e3e6-59d4-b281-a63c94591f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385848 #edb/ref 1080 "$3$ and $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385848 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385848 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40049e3f-bffa-5f0e-a9c8-2bba4686c07e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385849 #edb/ref 1080 "$9$ and $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385849 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385849 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085d1d56-b1dd-5cbd-a4fb-cac8147c91c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385850 #edb/ref 1080 "$2$ and $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385850 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385850 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "96138058-3bf5-5f68-b326-4f584f2e0377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385851 #edb/ref 1080 "$12$ and $9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385851 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385851 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a00dcda-5aaf-5a42-9073-65e57806a12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b93df85-0b3c-5ded-8201-a97d95285f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 